From 99e9eba235a62cfb2490c0695e2afee5c0adc932 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Javier Maestro <jjmaestro@ieee.org>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 19:42:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] patch: build on stable Rust

0.4.0 asks for `nightly-2024-09-14` in `rust-toolchain.toml` and gates three
compiler features. Two of them no longer exist: `avx512_target_feature` and
`stdarch_x86_avx512` were both stabilized in Rust 1.89, and on a stable compiler
naming a stabilized feature is a hard error rather than a no-op (E0554, "the
feature has been stable since 1.89.0 and no longer requires an attribute to
enable"). Dropping those two attributes is all it takes: every AVX-512
intrinsic behind them, and `#[target_feature(enable = "avx512...")]` itself,
compiles as it is.

The third, `stdarch_x86_avx512_f16`, is still unstable (rust #127213), so the
code that needs it goes: `reduce_sum_of_xy_v4_avx512fp16` and
`reduce_sum_of_d2_v4_avx512fp16`, their two tests, and their entries in the
`detect::multiversion` dispatch lists. They are the only two functions in the
extension that reach for an unstable API.

What that costs is one specialization on one class of hardware. `multiversion`
picks the best variant the CPU reports at runtime, and the two functions dropped
here are the `f16` distance kernels for CPUs with AVX-512 FP16 (Sapphire Rapids
and later), which compute in half precision natively. Those CPUs now take the
`v4` variant instead, which widens `f16` to `f32` and reduces in AVX-512 single
precision, so the answers stay within the same tolerance the deleted tests
asserted against the scalar fallback. Every other CPU is unaffected: `v4`, `v3`,
`v2`, `neon` and the fallback are untouched, and so is `f32` throughout.

References: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/127213
---
 crates/base/src/lib.rs         |   3 -
 crates/base/src/scalar/f16.rs  | 126 +--------------------------------
 crates/quantization/src/lib.rs |   2 -
 3 files changed, 2 insertions(+), 129 deletions(-)

